lpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par }\pard \qc\sl480\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs96 TEEN TITANS}{\fs96 \par }{\fs20 \page }{\b\fs48 CONTENTS\par }\pard \sl720\slmult0\widctlpar\tx8208\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 THE DEAL\tab 3\par THE GOOD GUYS\tab 4\par }\pard \li720\sl720\slmult0\widctlpar\tx8208\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 ROBIN\tab 5\par STARFIRE\tab 6\par CYBORG\tab 7\par RAVEN\tab 8\par BEAST BOY\tab 9\par }\pard \sl720\slmult0\widctlpar\tx8208\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 THE BAD GUYS\tab 10\par THE PLACES\tab 15\par THE STORIES\tab 17\par }\pard \li720\sl720\slmult0\widctlpar\tx8208\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 EVIL PLOTS\tab 17\par "DRAMA"\tab 19\par MOMENTS AND RUNNERS\tab 21\par }\pard \sl720\slmult0\widctlpar\tx8208\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 THE WRAP UP\tab 22\par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\b\fs20 THE DEAL\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par Five teenage superheroes live in a giant T-shaped tower on an Alcatraz-style island near Jump City, California.  When did they team-up?  How did they get their powers?  Why do they live together in a giant "T"?\par \par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\i\fs20 WHO CARES?!\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par Think about it:  you're in elementary school, you race home to watch T.V.  Do you really wanna sit around }{\i\fs20 learning}{\fs20  (yuck!) about }{\i\fs20 how}{\fs20  our heroes came fight crime, or do you just wanna watch 'em }{\i\fs20 fight crime?!}{\fs20   And that's what our heroes do -- in spades.\par \par Anything and everything that goes wrong in Jump City (and sometimes elsewhere in the world) is Teen Titans territory:  crooks and criminals, cape-and-tights supervillains, giant monsters, body-snatching aliens, earthquakes, floods, fires, radioactive hellbeasts from Dimension X -- you name it, they'll take it down.  The only rules:  the bad guy's gotta be cool and the threat's gotta be real.  There's plenty of great wacky-villain shows out there -- but this ain't one of 'em.\par \par But it's not a gloom-and-doom, serious superhero show either.  This is light-and-bright, clean-and-colorful, bad-guy-busting fun in a world of "mod" designs, West-Side-Story skies, anime action, and Hong Kong camera angles.  And our shows aren't just twenty-two minute slug-fests -- they're packed chock full of rich, creamy character comedy.  Sure, we've got five superheroes living in a giant T.  But we've also got five TEENAGERS living under one roof.  Which means (a la 'The Real World') somebody's always having 'drama' -- and on }{\i\fs20 this}{\fs20  show, 'drama' equals comedy.  And speaking of, let's meet ...\par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\b\fs20 THE GOOD GUYS}{\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par Backstories?  Who needs 'em?!  Alter egos?  <Pfff!>\par \par If }{\i\fs20 you}{\fs20  were a kid and }{\i\fs20 you}{\fs20  were a superhero -- would you really mess around with a secret identity?  No bleepin' way.  You'd wear that costume all-day-every-day and you'd use your hero name all the time.  So that's what we've got here.  Our heroes are Robin, Starfire, Cyborg, Beast Boy, and Raven -- straight up, that's all.  No questions asked.  (Rule #1:  if it can't pass the "WHO CARES?" test -- it's OUT.)\par \par Same goes for their origins.  Sure, they all got their powers }{\i\fs20 somehow}{\fs20  -- but that's in the }{\i\fs20 past}{\fs20 .  And just like real teens, the Titans only care about the }{\i\fs20 here}{\fs20  and }{\i\fs20 now.}{\fs20   Cyborg's not sitting around moping about the terrible accident years ago that left him a mechanized freak of nature -- he's pacing the floor }{\i\fs20 ranting}{\fs20  about the terrible accident five minutes ago that busted his brand new }{\i\fs20 Gamestation!}{\fs20 \par \par Why cast off all that great backstory and alter ego fun?  Because it doesn't tell us }{\i\fs20 who}{\fs20  the Titans really }{\i\fs20 are}{\fs20 .  Think back to when you met your best friend in school.  Did you need to know their middle name and where they were born to like them -- or was the fact that you both hated the same }{\i\fs20 lame}{\fs20  TV show enough to make you friends for life?  All that backstory stuff is who the Titans }{\i\fs20 were}{\fs20 .  Who they }{\i\fs20 are}{\fs20  is defined }{\i\fs20 in the now}{\fs20  -- by their actions, their attitudes, their quirks and pet peeves.  The Titans aren't just heroes; they're }{\i\fs20 people}{\fs20 .  And }{\i\fs20 that's}{\fs20  what makes them cool, interesting, and fun.\par \par Alright, roll call:\par \page }{\b\fs20 ROBIN}{\fs20 \par Get ready for a whole new Boy Wonder.  For one thing, DON'T call him that.  }{\i\fs20 This}{\fs20  Robin is a hard-charging, rough-and-tumble, cool-as-ice action hero.  He's one part Bruce Willis in 'Die Hard', one part Tony Hawk, with a generous splash of Jet Li.  He's a teenage version of Calvin (sans Hobbes).  Drive out to the suburbs, walk around any mall, and you'll see him.  He's every single kid out there with the big shoes, the spikey hair and the ready-for-anything attitude.\par \par Kung-fu trained, agile as all get out, and packing a belt-load of cool Batman-issue spy gadgets, he's a one-man SWAT team.  But at the end of the day, Robin has only one superpower:  he's crazy.  He'll leap before he looks -- literally.  He's got guts out the waz and he's not afraid of anything.  Furthermore, Robin's the resident problem-solver of the group.  He's probably a genius -- and not in the nerdy-glasses-guy kinda way; in the cool, homemade, jerry-rigged garage-band kinda way.  He's never read an instruction manual in his life -- but if you give him a puzzle, he won't stop 'til he finds a solution.  And }{\i\fs20 however}{\fs20  he comes up with them -- Robin's solutions are almost always right.\par \par Robin's only weakness:  he }{\i\fs20 never}{\fs20  }{\i\fs20 ever}{\fs20  gives up.  Sure, relentless determination is great when a giant ooze ogre's attacking the city -- but when you're just sitting around playing a board game, Robin's 'never say die' attitude can be a bit much.  He can't walk away from a dare, can't turn his back on a challenge, and has to win every argument.  Like any kid his age, Robin }{\i\fs20 hates}{\fs20  being underestimated:  }{\i\fs20 he}{\fs20  knows can handle anything that comes his way -- and he'll jump at any chance to }{\i\fs20 prove}{\fs20  it.  Robin's got the brains, the attitude, and the drive to be the Titans' leader -- but he }{\i\fs20 might}{\fs20  wanna work on his 'people skills' a little ...\par }{\b\fs20 \page STARFIRE}{\fs20 \par Simply put:  Star's a bodacious hottie from outer space.  But we won't be playing the 'princess' card with her or doing the "Oo!  I broke a nail!" gag.  Starfire has NO CLUE how pretty she is (on her world, she was considered quite plain.)  Truth told:  she has no clue about ANYTHING on this planet.  She's not stupid -- she's just an alien.  Starfire is the misfit who DESPERATELY wants to fit in.  For example, when the gang gets ice cream cones, Starfire finishes the ice cream and prepares to throw out 'the container' (i.e. the cone) until Robin explains you can EAT the 'container'.  Then later, when they get Chinese take-out -- everyone looks at Starfire like she's nuts as she starts eating all the cardboard boxes.  Earnest, fun-loving, bubbly, and emotionally fragile, Starfire is the conscience of our group.  Raven may be an empath -- but it's Starfire who identifies with everyone.  She's Jeanie from "I Dream of Jeanie", she's a grown up Bubbles from "Powerpuff Girls".\par \par Superpowers-wise:  Starfire can fly, she can shoot STARBOLTS from her hands, and she's virtually indestructible.  But since EVERYONE where she's from has these powers -- she doesn't quite get why this makes her special (and as our series goes on, Star may non-chalantly unveil a few cool new powers -- much to the surprise of her fellow Titans:  "You can shoot LASER BEAMS from your EYES???!"  An embarrassed Star:  "Um ... you can't?")\par }{\b\fs20 \page CYBORG\par }{\fs20 Everybody loves a bad boy.  Cyborg is our big, strong, in-your-face, bad-to-the-bone, super-powered robot tough guy -- and that's the way he LIKES it.  He's Arnold in 'T2', Ving Rhames in 'Pulp Fiction', Vin Diesel in anything ('cept Iron Giant).  Cyborg's the one who charges into battle with a grin on his face, the one who quips before he clobbers the bad guys.  If the Titans was a football team, he'd be the center.\par \par But he's not all tough-as-nails circuitry.  Deep down, he's kinda sensitive.  Sure the robot thing is cool, but it can be a drag, too.  When he gets in a car, it tips.  When they go to the beach, he sinks.  When he walks down the street, everyone stares (they stare at Starfire, too, but for }{\i\fs20 very}{\fs20  different reasons.)  And for all his bravado, he's honestly a bit of a neat freak.  Oh, and if you sit him down in front of the right tear-jerker chic-flick and actually make him watch -- Niagara Falls, baby!  Like any good Tin Man, Cyborg's all heart.}{\b\fs20 \par \par }{\fs20 Powers: he's crazy-strong, loaded with all sorts of cool gadgets and weapons that ratchet out of hidden compartments, he can jack into most computers, send out a taser-like shock, and his hand transforms into a SONIC CANNON (think:  earthquake-in-a-can.)  And he's the only Titan whose powers are upgradable.\par \page }{\b\fs20 RAVEN}{\fs20 \par And if Cyborg's our bad boy, Raven's our bad girl.  Or at least that's what she's afraid of.  With a shadowy past, eerie powers, and a bad reputation, Raven's }{\i\fs20 trying}{\fs20  to be good -- but she's desperately afraid that, deep down, she's pure darkness.  Raven KNOWS first hand about the evil that exists in the world (thanks to her VISIONS -- she sees it all the time.)  As a result, the only emotion she can feel is a sort of horrified disgust -- so she tries not to feel anything at all.  She's half Wednesday Addams and half Mr. Spock with a spoonful of 'Carrie'.  Sure, Raven's a smokin' super-babe just like Starfire -- but she's all-business, all-the-time, and she NEVER giggles.  She doesn't even smile (making her a perfect deadpan foil for Beast Boy's lame jokes.)\par \par As far as powers go, Raven's got a bundle.  But she's the only Titan who doesn't quite have control them.  In our series, Raven will be moving away from her 'Mistress of Magic' identity in the comic books toward a more 'ESP' kinda thing.  She won't be flipping tarot cards, casting bones, or working spells.  She'll be melting stuff just by thinking about it, seeing the future in tumultuous visions that nearly knock her out, and using her astral form to possess people or move objects.  Name anything ESP-related; and Raven }{\i\fs20 might}{\fs20  be able to do it -- question is:  can she control it?\par \par And one more thing, beneath that all-business facade, Raven's got a TEMPER.  And when she looses it, all hell breaks loose -- literally (think:  Carrie meets Firestarter meets Willow on last season's 'Buffy')  Very cool, very creepy.\par \par \page }{\b\fs20 BEAST BOY\par }{\fs20 Beast Boy is our class clown -- with all that entails.  Quick with his tongue and ravenously insecure, Beast Boy would do anything for a laugh -- }{\i\fs20 anything.}{\fs20   All he really wants is to be cool (which he's convinced that he's not -- he is GREEN, after all) and for Double B, humor is the weapon of choice.  He's Anthony Michael Hall in 'Sixteen Candles' with just a touch of Seth Green in 'Can't Hardly Wait'  He's the first to try out the fads and the last one to give them up.  If this was the Eighties (and let's all take a moment to give thanks that it's not), he'd be the kid who actually WORE a sequined glove.\par \par Beast Boy will provide plenty of comic relief -- sometimes we'll be laughing with him, and most of the time, we'll be laughing at him.  So what keeps BB from being the butt of all our jokes?\par \par His powers are pretty cool.  He can turn into any animal -- and in animal form, he can't talk (which should keep his jokeiness from getting annoying) AND ... he's a bad-ass.  When he's a rhino, he's every bit as brave and every bit as nasty.  When he's a lion, he's powerful and regal.  (And when he 'goes human' again, it always takes him a moment to shake off his animal 'tude.)  But what really makes Beast Boy cool is that, more than anyone else in the gang, he }{\i\fs20 really}{\fs20  loves his friends.  He'd never come out and }{\i\fs20 say}{\fs20  how much he cares.  But if push came to shove, Beast Boy would be the first to give up his life to save his friends.  He's kinda noble -- and when he drops the jokeman facade, much cooler than he realizes.\par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\b\fs20 THE BAD GUYS\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par Supervillains.  When there's trouble in the city -- these super-powered scoundrels are almost always to blame.  They come in all flavors:  a muscle-bound man-monster, a space-warping superthief, a giant oozing ogre, a spellbinding sorceress, a gadget-clad boy genius, and many more.  Most will be pulled from the DC Universe (anyone who first appeared in the }{\i\fs20 Teen Titans}{\fs20  or }{\i\fs20 Batman}{\fs20  comics is fair game except for the caped crusader's big foes:  Joker, Riddler, Catwoman, Penguin, etc.)  And when needed, we'll make up a few of our own.  The only rule:  they've gotta pass ...\par \par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20\ul THE SQUINT TEST\par }{\fs20 If ya hafta think, it probably stinks.\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par Who the bad guy is, what makes them cool, why they're a threat -- all of that has to be crystal clear }{\i\fs20 the second we lay eyes on them}{\fs20 .  If we don't "get it" right away -- it's not worth explaining.  A guy called "Vector" who has superhumanly perfect aim because of his ability to do complex math in his head ever since blah-blah-blah-boring-backstory would }{\i\fs20 fail}{\fs20  the Squint Test.  But a guy called "Warp" who can teleport anything he touches would }{\i\fs20 pass.\par \par }{\fs20 Ready to meet a few of our nasties?  (Careful, some of 'em bite.)}{\b\fs20 \par }{\fs20 \page }{\b\fs20 SLADE\par }{\fs20 It's as simple as this:  Slade is pure evil.  He's the shadowy mastermind pulling the strings.  He's the Emperor in 'Empire', the kitty-cat petting man from SPECTRE, the monster under your bed in an Armani suit.  In fact, Slade is }{\i\fs20 so}{\fs20  evil we'll never even see his }{\i\fs20 face}{\fs20   (well, never say }{\i\fs20 never}{\fs20  ...)  All we'll see is a dark silhouette, a powerful hand in black leather gauntlet, and the ancient medallion that he toys with while cooking up his villainous schemes.  How'd he get so evil?  }{\i\fs20 Who cares?!}{\fs20   He's a bad man; he's out to take over the city; and he'll DESTROY the Titans if they get in his way (he'll }{\i\fs20 try }{\fs20 anyway ...)  Though he's the main villain of our series, Slade doesn't do any fighting (yet).  But from the darkness of his secret compound, Slade is the driving force behind many of our plots -- forcing other supervillains to do his dirty work.  And when his hired guns fail and need to be punished, Slade calls on ...}{\b\fs20 \par \par WINTERGREEN}{\fs20 \par Slade's blue-skinned, four-armed manservant.  Silent, efficient, deadly:  think of him as the "Anti-Alfred."  We'll never say it, but Wintergreen is a genie.  He won't be granting wishes, popping out of a bottle or baring his midriff -- but he }{\i\fs20 does}{\fs20  have the power to produce whatever his master requires (in a 'slight of hand' kind of way).  When Slade needs tea, Wintergreen arrives with a shimmering silver tea service before his master has a chance to ask.  And when Slade needs a bodyguard, Wintergreen pulls four gleaming sabers out of his mouth and fights to the finish.\par \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 BLOCKBUSTER\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\keep\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20 Told}{\fs20  ya some of 'em bite ...  Blockbuster's an 8-foot-tall muscle-bound man-beast with more testosterone than the whole WWF and the I.Q. of a Rottweiler.  His own evil plans rarely consist of more than "Blockbuster SMASH!!" -- but for the right amount of steak, he's happy to carry out Slade's orders (or anyone else's.)\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par }{\b\fs20 PLASMUS}{\fs20 \par What's worse than a hideous 30-foot-tall ogre?  A hideous 30-foot-tall ogre with a }{\i\fs20 nasty}{\fs20  skin condition.  Plasmus is your worst zit come to life.  If the clawed hands and massive feet don't get you, the acid ooze that spews from his pores will.  And if you survive the ooze, don't celebrate yet -- his icky body comes apart.  His various limbs sprout fangs and pinchers to attack on their own.  Although he's not much of a talker, Plasmus is no dummy (there's actually }{\i\fs20 human}{\fs20  somewhere in all that goo.)  Like any predator, he's cunning enough to challenge his prey.\par \par }{\b\fs20 GIZMO}{\fs20 \par Covered from head-to-toe in cool high-tech gadgets, the evil boy genius known as Gizmo is the supervillain equivalent of everybody's bratty kid brother.  Small, quick, and wily, Gizmo can make a high-tech weapon out of any technology he gets his hands on -- fast.  In a flash of fidgeting, a cellphone becomes a laser gun, a security camera becomes a force beam projector, a palm pilot becomes a tiny-but-ferocious remote-controlled attack robot, etc.  Like any egotistical know-it-all, he }{\i\fs20 loves}{\fs20  to prove he's the smartest person in the room.  But Gizmo doesn't stop there -- he's out to prove he's the smartest person on the }{\i\fs20 planet!}{\fs20 \par \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 MAMMOTH}{\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\keep\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 This meaty man-boy isn't just big for his age -- he's big for his }{\i\fs20 species!}{\fs20   With unstoppable strength and muscles for brains, Mammoth is an exaggerated version of everybody's school bully.  And like any bully, he's not satisfied with simply defeating his opponents -- he wants to }{\i\fs20 humiliate}{\fs20  them.  For example, Mammoth might pop off one of Cyborg's robot arms -- and then taunt him with it, holding it just out of his reach.  But whatever you do, don't turn your back on him:  Mammoth gives one }{\i\fs20 heck}{\fs20  of a wedgie!\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par }{\b\fs20 JINX}{\fs20 \par Beautiful, arrogant, evil:  the teenage girl known as Jinx is just a complete-and-total raging witch.  If this was high school, she'd be the sort-of-popular girl who thinks she's }{\i\fs20 the}{\fs20  popular girl.  Armed with an arsenal of magic-word-driven spells, Jinx can shoot flaming blasts from her hands, conjure menacing monsters, and turn just about anyone into a toad.  But her spells only last as long as she's around to keep them from breaking -- when Jinx moves "out of range" or gets distracted, everything goes back to normal.\par \par }{\b\fs20 WARP\par }{\fs20 This slick superthief has perfected the clean getaway.  With a teleporting techo-suit on his side, Warp can zap himself in and out of a bank vault before anyone even gets a chance to scream.  And if you }{\i\fs20 do}{\fs20  get your hand on him, watch out:  Warp can teleport anything he touches.  He might zap you up a hundred feet in the air -- or bury you hundred feet underground!  Luckily, Warp can't teleport very }{\i\fs20 far}{\fs20 ,  so he's not }{\i\fs20 impossible}{\fs20  to chase, just }{\i\fs20 very hard}{\fs20  to}{\i\fs20  }{\fs20 catch.  Personality-wise, Warp is a smug, smarmy show-off.  He'd probably get away with a lot more robberies if he wasn't so obsessed with making sure everybody }{\i\fs20 knows}{\fs20  he did it.\par }{\b\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 KILLER MOTH\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\keep\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 The crime-world's answer to Batman, Killer Moth is just an ordinary guy who's taken it upon himself to get out there and fight for what's wrong.  With incredible skill in hand-to-hand combat, a sensor-equipped helmet, and a utility belt to rival Robin's -- Killer Moth is serious trouble and a formidable foe.  And he's also }{\i\fs20 really}{\fs20  bossy!  Since he's older than the Titans, Moth thinks }{\i\fs20 he}{\fs20  should be able to tell them what to do.  Remember that nasty school vice principal who }{\i\fs20 loved}{\fs20  ordering people around?  There was probably a Killer Moth costume hanging in his closet.}{\b\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 \par THUNDER AND LIGHTNING\par }{\fs20 Forces of nature made flesh, Thunder and Lightning aren't }{\i\fs20 evil}{\fs20  -- they're just a little ... }{\i\fs20 rambunctious}{\fs20 .  They have awesome powers:  Thunder's earthshattering sonic booms can crumble concrete, bend steel, and knock you on your butt; and Lightning's electrical blasts make him the world's biggest taser.  And like a couple of teenage vandals, these brothers have a }{\i\fs20 blast}{\fs20  (literally) wrecking anything they get their hands on.\par \par }{\b\fs20 PSIMON}{\fs20 \par Sometimes appearance says it all:  here's a guy who's so proud of his brain -- he put in a }{\i\fs20 sunroof}{\fs20  so everybody can see it.  Psimon is the self-satisfied, all-knowing authority figure.  If he was a teacher, he'd be the one who loved giving pop quizzes.  In addition to his big brain's impressive ability to concoct evil schemes, Psimon's mind powers allow him to levitate objects, read minds, and control people's thoughts.\par \par }{\b\fs20 AND MANY, MANY MORE ...}{\fs20 \par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\b\fs20 THE PLACES\par }\pard \sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 \par JUMP CITY\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 Jump City is San Fran, L.A., San Diego, and all things Cali rolled into one gigantic, sun-drenched megalopolis.  It's got everything you could ever need:  a beautiful bay spanned by the monumental Jump City Bridge, the 'Walk of Stars' and the famous white-lettered 'GLITTERLAND' sign, amusement parks galore and a humongous zoo, outdoor malls, parks, beaches, subways, freeways, downtown, uptown, Chinatown -- }{\i\fs20 everything.}{\fs20   And in the middle of it all, we find ...\par \par }{\b\fs20 TITANS TOWER}{\fs20 \par On an Alcatraz-style island in center of the bay, a ten-story T-shaped glass-and-steel high-rise is home to the coolest heroes Jump City has ever known.  Serving as headquarters, home, and hangout, Titans Tower has plenty of room for everything a gang of crime-fighting teens could ever need.  There's a HANGAR for the Titans' HOVERJET, a GARAGE for Robin's HOT ROD and Cyborg's SUPERCYCLE, and a SUB-BASEMENT AIRLOCK for the Titans' SUBMARINE.  There's a ROOF-TOP POOL for outdoor relaxation, a KITCHEN where no one }{\i\fs20 ever}{\fs20  seems to do the dishes, and a super-powered GYM with all sorts of cool, character-specific work-out gear (e.g. really heavy weights for Cyborg, parallel bars and a wooden man for Robin, etc.)  And of course, each of the Titans has their own room -- decorated to reflect their personal style.\par \par But the center of the Titans' universe is the high-tech OPERATIONS CENTER that does double duty as the gang's main hangout.  The briefing area looks remarkably like a sunken living room with a big loungable c-shaped sofa (and a 'coffee table' that's actually a sophisticated holographic projector.)  The computer consoles that flank the room are angled just right for a little indoor half-pipe skating.  And the massive window that spans the room isn't }{\i\fs20 just}{\fs20  for a panoramic view of the city -- it's also the biggest T.V. SCREEN you've ever seen.  It's perfect for video-games, channel-flipping, or movie night -- oh yeah, and it's pretty helpful for crime-fighting-y detective stuff too.\par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 \page THE STORIES\par }\pard \sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 Okay:  got heroes, got villains, got a city full o' troubles and a cool T-shaped headquarters ... let's see some action!\par \par }\pard \sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20 EVIL PLOTS\par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 Now, we wanna cram as much thrills, excitement, and fun into our 22 minutes as possible, so simplicity is our friend.  We don't need complicated James-Bond-esque threats that require a grounding in international politics and quantum theory to understand.  Our villains can't have hard-to-follow motives that only make sense if you've read a big stack of comic books.  And we don't want plots with enough little twists to put a crick in your neck (a few BIG ones will do fine.)\par \par But just because we're doing away with complexity }{\i\fs20 doesn't}{\fs20  mean we won't have great stories.  The action's gotta escalate, our outcomes hafta be unpredictable, and our plots must thicken.  We've just gotta get there by thinking with our }{\i\fs20 eyes}{\fs20 .  Anything we can't }{\i\fs20 see}{\fs20  -- won't work.  Everything we }{\i\fs20 can}{\fs20  see needs to be strange, exciting, and full of surprises.  We're looking for:\par \par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 VISUAL -- }{\b\fs20 NOT}{\fs20  VERBAL\par IMAGINATIVE -- }{\b\fs20 NOT}{\fs20  COMPLICATED\par }{\b\fs20 SIMPLE}{\fs20  -- BUT }{\b\fs20 WEIRD, CRAZY, AND COOL}{\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par Yup:  the Squint Test applies to stories, too.  If you've got an idea about a villain who's stealing high-tech components to assemble a radioactive doomsday machine, save it.  If your idea is about a villain who's }{\i\fs20 made}{\fs20  of high-tech stuff and he shoots electronic worms from his fingertips and the worms get in your brain and make giant robots grow out of your nose ... you might be on to something.  The schemes have to be }{\i\fs20 clearly}{\fs20  evil -- stuff that makes your inner 8-year-old stand up and shout:  "No }{\i\fs20 way!}{\fs20   We gotta }{\i\fs20 stop 'em!}{\fs20 "  Our villains don't smuggle explosives or manipulate the stock market.  They turn people to stone and steal the super-cool-high-tech car!  If you have to explain }{\i\fs20 why}{\fs20  it's wrong -- it's not wrong enough.\par \par Here's a few springboards to get us started:\par \par > SLADE unleashes PLASMUS to bring Jump City to its knees.  And it's all the Titans can do to keep the giant oozing ogre at bay.  But when Plasmus separates into five separate-but-equally-dangerous monsters, will our heroes be able to stop him -- or will }{\i\fs20 they}{\fs20  fall to pieces, too?\par \par > At a special school for young supervillains, MAMMOTH, GIZMO, and JINX are given the assignment for their final exam:  DESTROY THE TITANS.  Will our heroes be able to protect each other from this well trained trio of super-powered assassins?\par \par > When THUNDER AND LIGHTNING come to town, their vandalizing 'fun' threatens to level the city.  Can the Titans get the sinister siblings to stop -- or will Slade take the stormy brothers under his wing?\par \par > A super-cool-high-tech car is unveiled at the Jump City Expo.  But before can take it for a test drive -- WARP zaps in and steals it!  We're in for a wild ride as the Titans work to bring the Warp-driven super-car back without a scratch.\par \par >  MAMMOTH claims the coolest part of town as his "Stomping Grounds" and drives everyone else away.  Can the Titans move a man-mountain to take back their turf?\par }\pard \sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\b\fs20 "DRAMA"}{\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 But our shows aren't }{\i\fs20 just}{\fs20  cool villains, evil plots, and thrilling action.  With five teenagers living under one roof, we've got lots of 'drama', too.  And 'drama' almost always leads to comedy.\par \par Every episode needs a strong, character-driven, kid-relatable conflict -- preferably one we can play for laughs (and a little 'heart' never hurts either.)  Any problems that }{\i\fs20 real}{\fs20  kids have to deal with is fair game:  getting in a fight with your friend, losing your favorite toy, being forced to do something you don't wanna do, accidentally breaking something and then trying to cover it up, etc.  As much fun as the action is, }{\i\fs20 these}{\fs20  stories are the hook that makes us }{\i\fs20 care}{\fs20  about the Titans, the thing that grounds our fantasy-filled show in recognizable reality.  (And remember, our heroes are a }{\i\fs20 team}{\fs20 :  one person's problem is }{\i\fs20 everyone's}{\fs20  problem -- we'll have lots of fun tracking the other Titans' attitudes and reactions to what's going on.)  A few examples:\par \par >  On a mission, Robin and Cyborg accidentally get in each other's way.  And when neither of them will apologize, they get in a big fight -- and Cyborg quits the team!  Can the Titans still save the city with one of their members missing?\par \par >  Starfire's devious older sister BLACKFIRE comes to visit; and she immediately hits it off with the other Titans.  Blackfire seems to fit in }{\i\fs20 so}{\fs20  much better ... that misfit Starfire worries she's going to be replaced!\par \par >  When Beast Boy and Cyborg get curious about }{\i\fs20 who}{\fs20  Raven is and }{\i\fs20 where}{\fs20  she comes from, Raven's not exactly forthcoming with information.  So they launch a secret investigation into their friend.  And their investigation accidentally opens up a portal to Raven's }{\i\fs20 mind}{\fs20  -- where they wind up going hand-to-hand with Raven's personal demons!\par \par >  Beast Boy introduces Starfire to the joys of chocolate, and she realizes a little too late that what humans call chocolate is actually "grebnak" -- the Tamaranian equivalent of a LOVE POTION.  Star goes 'boy crazy' for the rest of the episode.\par \page }{\b\fs20 MOMENTS AND RUNNERS}{\fs20 \par But we're not stopin' at }{\i\fs20 just}{\fs20  a villain-driven action-story and a hero-driven character-story.  Oh no.  We're gonna trick these puppies out with all the funny moments, running gags and sitcom-esque comic threads we can cram between the credits.  Here's a few ideas to get us started:\par \par >  The new Gamestation system is about to come out and Cyborg and Beast Boy have been waiting in line outside the SuperBuy for three days to get one before they sell out.  But when the mission call comes, will they }{\i\fs20 really}{\fs20  give up their spot -- or will they 'tag team' their way through the rest of the episode to keep their place in line?\par \par >  An all-nite movie marathon keeps the Titans up all night, some voluntarily, others }{\i\fs20 in}{\fs20 voluntarily.\par \par >  Beast Boy gets a huge zit that's always there no matter WHAT he morphs into.  And he spends most of the mission trying to pop it.\par \par >  Beast Boy, Cyborg, and Robin have an ongoing belching contest -- that RAVEN ultimately wins!\par \par >  After a trip to the mall introduces Starfire to the strange concept of 'make up' -- the intimidated beauty goes around wearing WAY WAY WAY too much.\par \par >  When the flu bug comes to town, the whole team winds up fighting a cold -- at the same time they're trying to fight bad guys.  "Freeze and put your hands-- AACHOOO!"\par \par >  When Beast Boy gets hold of a video camera, he decides to make a movie with his friends as the stars -- whether they want to be in the picture or not.\par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\b\i\fs20 Cool Heroes\par Nasty Villains\par Slam-Bang Action\par Hilarious Jokes}{\b\fs20 \par }{\fs20 \par In other words ...\par \par }{\b\fs96 FUN}{\b\fs20 \par }\pard \sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \par }\pard \qj\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 The Teen Titans may be out to save the world -- but we're just out to give kids one whopper of great time.  Childhood isn't easy:  there's stupid rules you don't wanna follow, boring homework you don't wanna do, yucky vegetables you don't wanna eat, yummy ice cream in freezers you can't reach, and a whole buncha stuff you just don't understand.\par \par And the best part of childhood is the stuff that's made }{\i\fs20 just}{\fs20  }{\i\fs20 for you}{\fs20 :  toys you like, a treehouse that's too small for grown-ups, cool kid-sneakers that light up when you walk, pizza places with video games and a ball room, comic books.  And the best stuff on T.V. is the stuff that's there }{\i\fs20 just for you}{\fs20 .\par \par With Hip young superheroes, awesome super powers, plots kids can understand, character stories kids can relate to and loads of exciting eye-candy -- }{\i\fs20 Teen Titans}{\fs20  is half-hour long banana split for your brain, a roller coaster you can ride in your living room.  Sure, there'll be positive role models, lessons about right and wrong, and all that good stuff -- but who cares?!  You don't think about all that nutritious potassium you're getting when you eat a banana split, do ya?  All that matters is ...\par \par }\pard \qc\sl360\slmult0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 When you watch }{\i\fs20 Teen Titans}{\fs20  -- you have }{\b\fs20 FUN!!}{\fs20 \par }}